Hi Andrew,

We unfortunately dropped the ball on your reply mostly because priorities
have changed and I just couldn't spend time on this. Hopefully it changes
in the future and in the past few days/weeks I revisited the KIP and saw
your reply.

So let me answer it:

AS1: The figure is meant to represent things on a conceptual level, but I
replaced it with a figure that represents the actual idea.

AS2: the thought behind binding a single user to a single virtual cluster
was that this way virtual clusters can be totally transparent to them and
there will be no need to specify which virtual cluster it wants to use.
Therefore topics don't have to be prepended with the name of the virtual
cluster. This also implies that there would be no need to change client
protocols to include the virtual cluster feature and would be totally
backward compatible.
Then with topic linking a topic could be shared between virtual clusters,
so there would be no need for a user to belong to more virtual clusters. If
we allow users to span virtual clusters, then:
(1) the feature can't stay transparent anymore as clients would have to
specify the virtual cluster. This also means that to use this feature,
clients would need to be upgraded to a version which supports virtual
clusters. This often slows down the adoption of the feature as in large
organization clients are managed by independent teams.
(2) topic linking becomes redundant. It isn't a problem but is just a
different approach. If a user can join multiple VCs, then there is no
reason to share topics.
(3) I would say data sharing is more important than entity sharing. From a
practical point of view, when a Kafka deployment is shared between multiple
departments of an organization, it is much more common that they need to
access or modify common data but act as separate teams. This maps well to
virtual clusters where a user is assigned to a simple VC and the data is
shared between teams (VCs) with topic linking.

AS3: Yes, unfortunately this feature is quite heavily dependent on having
principals and ACLs, but I also recognize it is a fair point that spinning
it up for development should be easier. Perhaps we could provide
authenticators for development that take the client.id as principal, so
it's not needed to set up authentication on the clients.

AS4. Yes, this is a good improvement that is missing from the KIP indeed.
And I go one step further: we should be able to derive this information
from the principal itself as that has the user -> VC binding. Then after
doing the authorization checks (if needed) for an incoming client
connection, create the group or transactional ID as needed in the VC. Apart
from this though we can still extend the client tools with the
--virtual-cluster option, that may also help in some cases if manual
assignment is desired.

AS5. I think this connects to your previous point, right? In that case I
think principal based assignment would solve this.

AS6. You make a good point here, this way we would need to introduce naming
rules. Modifying the coordinator record schemas may be much harder than
prefixing as these records aren't defined as flexible. Especially after
KIP-848 it may require much heavier changes, solving backward
compatibility, etc.. However, instead of encoding the VC name, we could
introduce a VC UUID and use the "uuid.vcname.groupname" scheme in
__consumer_offsets' key. One would have to be very unlucky to use a similar
naming convention that is pre-VC. Also, given the principal in the request,
we can expect and control whether users can commit offsets for instance and
this way we could prevent malicious overrides. Although at this point
(without knowing the real extent of the schema modifications) feel that
might be a better approach as it's more robust, this alternative prefixing
is perhaps worth considering.

Best,
Viktor

